    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Mammalian orders that are highly speciose in the modern day tend to be comprised of species covering a broad range of feeding behaviors, as indicated by a diverse dentition. Yet the order Eulipotyphla, which includes the shrews, moles, and hedgehogs and relatives, all have teeth adapted for shearing, despite dietary specialization of the Eulipotyphlan families. To test the hypothesis that Eulipotyphlans pursue an alternative pathway to achieving dietary diversity by altering the amount of force they can apply with their jaws, I sampled specimens from across the order, photographing mandibles and measuring their mechanical potential, a metric of bite force. I found that there is highly significant between-family variation in mechanical potential in Eulipotyphlans; I also found this between-family diversity is tied to prey hardness. Further, there is high phylogenetic signal of mechanical potential, agreeing with family-specific results. To broaden my analyses, I compared the mechanical potential of modern Eulipotyphlans to two fossil groups: (1) two species of the earliest known mammaliaform fossils, which were also insectivorous and lived during the Jurassic, and (2) extinct stem-group Eulipotyphlans that lived in the Paleocene. My results tentatively suggest increasing bite force from the Jurassic to the Paleocene, such that these insectivorans appear to have adapted to apply greater force, possibly an adaptation to pierce through harder insect carapaces, thereby accessing a broad niche. This paper presents the first quantitative analysis that spans all of the families in the order, including one of the most recently extinct mammalian families, the Nesophontidae, and contributes to a growing body of biomechanics-based knowledge on mammalian dietary patterns.",
